1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:05,351
You give an agent a job, and it just works for a while. And then it stops.

2
00:00:05,601 --> 00:00:09,306
Maybe it finished. Maybe it asked you something an hour ago.

3
00:00:09,006 --> 00:00:11,520
You only find out when you go and look.

4
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:15,767
Crowsnest is one program that runs your coding agents,

5
00:00:15,467 --> 00:00:18,757
keeps them working, and tells you when they need you.

6
00:00:21,658 --> 00:00:24,094
Type what needs doing into one box.

7
00:00:26,344 --> 00:00:28,609
Crowsnest hands it to a new agent,

8
00:00:28,309 --> 00:00:31,845
in its own git worktree, and the job lands on the board.

9
00:00:33,295 --> 00:00:36,648
The board keeps everything in order: what needs you,

10
00:00:36,348 --> 00:00:39,818
what's stuck, what's running, and what finished today.

11
00:00:47,658 --> 00:00:51,261
An agent that ends its turn without saying done, blocked,

12
00:00:50,961 --> 00:00:54,042
or waiting on you is sent straight back to work.

13
00:00:55,091 --> 00:00:57,527
Every nudge is on the job's timeline.

14
00:01:00,158 --> 00:01:04,667
If it keeps stopping, or hits a usage limit, the job is marked stuck,

15
00:01:04,367 --> 00:01:07,656
with the cause, and the one button that fixes it.

16
00:01:13,158 --> 00:01:17,777
When an agent needs a decision, it asks, and the rest of the work carries on.

17
00:01:18,626 --> 00:01:22,595
Questions land in the Inbox, with a diagram or a diff when that helps.

18
00:01:26,158 --> 00:01:28,755
Tool permissions arrive as readable cards:

19
00:01:28,455 --> 00:01:31,381
the command, where it runs, and how risky it is.

20
00:01:32,430 --> 00:01:34,913
Allow it once, or deny it with a note.

21
00:01:37,658 --> 00:01:40,744
And the needs-you tray follows you onto every page.

22
00:01:46,658 --> 00:01:49,823
When a job is done, a second agent reviews it.

23
00:01:49,523 --> 00:01:54,992
The Review tab shows what it produced, file by file, beside the reviewer's verdict.

24
00:01:56,042 --> 00:01:58,859
Then land it: merge, open a pull request,

25
00:01:58,559 --> 00:02:01,683
or discard it, and its worktree is cleaned up.

26
00:02:05,658 --> 00:02:10,881
Each project gets its own lead agent, with its own jobs, schedules and instructions.

27
00:02:11,730 --> 00:02:14,259
Chat with the orchestrator, or with any lead.

28
00:02:15,709 --> 00:02:18,377
And Command-K gives a job, or jumps anywhere.

29
00:02:24,658 --> 00:02:26,758
On your phone it's the same app.

30
00:02:26,458 --> 00:02:30,020
Glance at the board, answer a question, approve a command.

31
00:02:35,269 --> 00:02:37,845
Push notifications tell you when something needs you.

32
00:02:40,745 --> 00:02:42,746
Hold to talk to the orchestrator.

33
00:02:42,446 --> 00:02:46,664
Replies come back as one spoken line, and each agent can have its own voice.

34
00:02:50,114 --> 00:02:54,501
And every project's lead shows up in Claude's Remote Control, under its own name.

35
00:02:57,401 --> 00:03:00,940
The fleet shows every agent in the session and what it's doing,

36
00:03:00,640 --> 00:03:03,460
and each one's terminal runs live in the browser.

37
00:03:09,901 --> 00:03:15,913
Spend is measured per job and per day. Schedules bring recurring work round on time,

38
00:03:15,613 --> 00:03:19,721
and the journal records every nudge, answer and restart.

39
00:03:20,571 --> 00:03:23,611
And the header shows the machine's load at a glance.

40
00:03:26,511 --> 00:03:29,597
Getting started is one install, and one command.

41
00:03:31,111 --> 00:03:34,069
Open the link it prints and pick a password.

42
00:03:33,769 --> 00:03:37,634
A short checklist confirms your tools, and you press Start.

43
00:03:44,111 --> 00:03:49,009
No more checking tabs to see who stopped. Give it a job. Crowsnest keeps it going.

